//! External blob-storage [`CheckpointStore`](super::super::CheckpointStore)
//! adapter.
//!
//! Owns a shared [`reqwest::Client`] constructed once at
//! [`StorageApiCheckpointStore::new`] time (mirroring
//! `RestStorageServiceClient::new` for the explicit connect-phase +
//! per-request timeout pair), the trimmed endpoint URL under which the
//! storage-api REST surface is reachable, the base path under which all
//! `.live-edit/` artifacts are addressed, and an
//! [`Arc<ServiceTokenProvider>`] outbound-auth handle shared with
//! `LiveEditService` and other adapter call sites.
//!
//! ## URL / path shape (SDD §SR-4.1 .. §SR-4.6)
//!
//! Every artifact resolves to a `by-address` URL of the shape
//! `<endpoint>/v1alpha/fileobject/by-address/<percent-encoded-address>`,
//! where `<address>` is composed from the operator-configured base path
//! and a fixed `.live-edit/` namespace prefix. The path-rendering
//! helpers (`live_edit_prefix`, `by_address_url`, `layer_ompf_url`, and
//! siblings) own that composition; they preserve the operator-configured
//! base path verbatim — normalizing only a trailing `/` so a documented
//! `BASE_PATH` ending in `/` does not produce a double-slash address — and
//! percent-encode the *entire* composed address (not just user-controlled
//! segments) through [`encode_resource_address`] — the same encoder the
//! auth lane uses for the same `by-address` endpoint — so a `/` byte inside a
//! `session_id` is encoded uniformly with the surrounding base-path bytes.
//! The base path is a full storage-api `by-address` resource — typically a
//! blob URL such as `https://host/container/live-edit-checkpoints/` — and is
//! emitted with its scheme intact, matching
//! `RestStorageServiceClient::check_permission`'s full-URL encoding contract.
//!
//! The address families under that namespace are:
//!
//! - `layers/<object_id>.<snap_seq>.ompf` — a **base** generation's payload,
//!   sharing its stem with the `layers/<object_id>.<snap_seq>.meta.json`
//!   companion and, when the assembler emitted one, the
//!   `layers/<object_id>.<snap_seq>.ext-refs.json` sidecar.
//! - `layers/<object_id>.<to_seq>.tail` — a **tail** generation's envelope,
//!   with the kind-qualified `layers/<object_id>.<to_seq>.tail.meta.json`
//!   companion. The `.tail` qualifier on the meta is load-bearing: SR-4.1
//!   re-base mints a base at the newest consumed tail's `to_seq`, so
//!   `base@S` and `tail@to_seq == S` genuinely coexist and an unqualified
//!   meta name would have one silently clobber the other. A tail carries
//!   **no** ext-refs sidecar (SR-2.4, enforced at the write side by
//!   `validate_tail_put`) — there is deliberately no address for one.
//! - `sessions/<session_id>.json` — the session checkpoint.
//! - `ext/<hash_hex>.blob` — a content-addressed external-value blob.
//! - `counters.json` — the singleton counters sidecar.
//!
//! The two generation families are addressed under one flat `layers/`
//! prefix, and the suffix partition between them is what lets a single
//! paginated LIST of `layers/` serve every layer enumeration on this
//! adapter — see the "Why the OMPF and tail parser families cannot alias"
//! note above [`parse_tail_filename`]. The `sessions/` listing is a separate
//! LIST; both run through one paginated walk,
//! `StorageApiCheckpointStore::list_subtree_filenames`.
//!
//! storage-api answers an Enumerate of a prefix that holds no objects with a
//! 404, on every page. The walk therefore reports a first-page 404 as
//! [`CheckpointError::NotFound`] and lets each listing decide what an empty
//! subtree means; a 404 on a later page means the subtree changed under the
//! walk and is `Transient`.
//!
//! ## Outbound auth (SDD §14.6)
//!
//! Every request authenticates with the sidecar-provisioned
//! service-identity Bearer token resolved through
//! [`ServiceTokenProvider`]. The provider is constructed in `main.rs`
//! when `LIVE_EDIT_SERVICE_TOKEN_FILE` is set and threaded into this
//! adapter by `build_checkpoint_store`. `Config::from_env` is the primary
//! gate: it refuses `StorageApi` without the token file, so the process
//! exits with a configuration error before any adapter exists. The
//! dispatcher also fails closed when it is handed no provider, as defense
//! in depth for a caller that bypasses that gate. Every send site reads
//! [`ServiceTokenProvider::cached_bearer_if_unexpired`] — synchronous, and
//! `None` only when nothing is cached or the cached token has *actually*
//! expired (b.e49; the same correction b.d6b made on the Valkey credential
//! path). The provider's `refresh_lead` is a proactive re-read cadence, not
//! a validity floor: withholding a still-good credential here bought nothing
//! but a guaranteed 401, because the request then goes out unauthenticated.
//! Failure-mode symmetry is what makes serving it safe — if storage-api
//! really would reject a near-expiry token, attaching it 401s exactly as
//! withholding it did, and that 401 already drives `force_refresh()` + one
//! retry. Where the two differ is the *verdict* on a second 401, and
//! deliberately so: a token the provider considers live being rejected twice
//! is a credential storage-api will not accept, which is an operator problem
//! rather than a rotation gap — see the second-401 section on
//! `send_classified`. `None` is still tolerated: callers in this adapter treat
//! it the same as a missing token and propagate the resulting 401 (or wrapped
//! error) up to the retry / classification surface.

