/// Maximum total attempts on Transient outcomes. Matches Task 5 conformance
/// case #19 (`t2.fgj.jd.yz`) which programs wiremock to return 503 twice then
/// 200 expecting success, and 503 four times expecting `Err(Transient)`.
const MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS: u32 = 3;

/// Number of outer auth attempts `send_classified` makes per logical request:
/// the initial send plus one post-`force_refresh()` retry on a 401.
///
/// Named rather than inlined because it is one of the three factors in
/// [`storage_api_publish_worst_case`], and the reconciliation grace derived from
/// that product is a safety bound — a reader checking the arithmetic should find
/// every factor spelled out, not two constants and a literal `2`.
const AUTH_ATTEMPTS: u32 = 2;

/// This adapter's **enforced** ceiling on the wall-clock cost of one logical
/// request — one [`StorageApiCheckpointStore::send_classified`] call, retries,
/// backoff sleeps, and the 401 `force_refresh` included.
///
/// `request_timeout` (`LIVE_EDIT_CHECKPOINT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS`, applied
/// per-request by `reqwest`) × [`MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`] transient retries ×
/// [`AUTH_ATTEMPTS`] auth attempts. At the defaults that is 30 s × 3 × 2 =
/// 180 s.
///
/// # Enforced, not merely reported (b.gg7)
///
/// Until b.gg7 this was a *description* of the retry product, and the product
/// was only a lower bound on reality: the two backoff sleeps and the
/// `force_refresh()` await sat outside it, and nothing stopped the sum. It is
/// now the argument to a `tokio::time::timeout` wrapped around the entire
/// two-loop body in [`StorageApiCheckpointStore::send_classified`] — the single
/// enforcement site — so those three terms are absorbed *inside* the number
/// rather than added to it. Everything downstream that consumes the bound (the
/// reconciliation grace, the retention cascade's residual window, the lease
/// arithmetic in `checkpoint::checkpointer`) is therefore reasoning about a
/// ceiling the code actually holds to.
///
/// The value is deliberately unchanged. b.gg7 asked for a *knowable* ceiling,
/// not a tighter default — the multi-MB blob rationale for the 30 s per-request
/// timeout (SR-4.2) stands, and shrinking the product would trade away
/// legitimate transient recovery. An elapse is classified
/// [`CheckpointError::Transient`]: a backend brownout that outlasts three
/// attempts is exactly the thing the next tick should retry.
///
/// Expressed as live arithmetic over the configured timeout rather than as a
/// baked constant: an operator who raises the request timeout raises this bound
/// with it, and the reconciliation grace that consumes it stays honest. A frozen
/// `180 s` would silently under-bound every deployment that tuned the timeout up.
///
/// A free function (not just the trait method) so `Config::from_env` can
/// validate `LIVE_EDIT_CHECKPOINT_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_SECS` against the same
/// arithmetic at startup, before any adapter has been constructed, and so
/// `register_checkpoint_pacing_gauges_if_enabled` can echo it as a config gauge.
pub(crate) fn storage_api_publish_worst_case(request_timeout: Duration) -> Duration {
    request_timeout.saturating_mul(MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS * AUTH_ATTEMPTS)
}

/// This adapter's ceiling on one **multi-request enumeration** — the paginated
/// LIST walk of the `layers/` or `sessions/` subtree, and the per-sidecar GET
/// fan-out that consumes the `layers/` walk (b.gg7).
///
/// [`storage_api_publish_worst_case`] × [`RECONCILE_GRACE_MULTIPLE`]: 540 s at
/// the defaults. An enumeration is a *sequence* of `send_classified` calls whose
/// length is data-dependent (pages of a subtree, sidecars of a fleet), so the
/// per-call ceiling bounds each element and this bounds the sequence.
///
/// # Why this multiple, and not a new knob
///
/// The multiple is borrowed rather than invented, and it carries the same
/// staleness argument [`reconcile_drain_budget`] makes for its own grace term:
/// every consumer of these enumerations acts on the answer as a *snapshot* of
/// what is live, and a snapshot older than one grace window is one nobody may
/// safely delete against — it re-opens exactly the race the grace exists to
/// close. So an enumeration that has already run a full grace window has not
/// produced a slow answer, it has produced an answer with no remaining use.
/// Stopping is strictly better than finishing.
///
/// Checked **between** calls: the deadline aborts the loop at a request
/// boundary. One ceiling inside the adapter covers every caller —
/// `list_layer_artifacts`, `list_all_object_ids`, `list_layer_checkpoints`,
/// `delete_layer_temp_artifacts`, `get_latest_layer_checkpoint` and
/// `list_session_checkpoint_ids` all funnel through the same pagination
/// primitive. The session-id listing's consumer (the DR restore Job) deletes
/// nothing against its answer, so the staleness argument above is not what
/// bounds it; it shares the ceiling because it shares the primitive, and a
/// walk that never ends is a hang whatever its consumer.
///
/// # Exact, not approximate (review round 2)
///
/// The between-calls test alone would make this number a *lower* bound: a page
/// or sidecar admitted at `deadline − ε` is entitled to a full
/// [`storage_api_publish_worst_case`] afterwards, so a walk's real cost would be
/// `ceiling + 180 s` — 720 s at the defaults against a published 540 s, and the
/// sidecar walk's "carries it twice" would understate by the same term twice
/// over. Every call inside these loops is therefore additionally clamped to
/// `min(publish_worst_case, deadline − now)` by
/// [`StorageApiCheckpointStore::send_classified_within`], which is what makes
/// the published figure the figure. The clamp is why these loops may drop an
/// in-flight request where the publish paths may not; see that method for the
/// (idempotent-read / idempotent-DELETE) argument.
///
/// [`reconcile_drain_budget`]: crate::checkpoint::checkpointer
/// [`RECONCILE_GRACE_MULTIPLE`]: crate::checkpoint::store::RECONCILE_GRACE_MULTIPLE
pub(crate) fn storage_api_enumeration_ceiling(request_timeout: Duration) -> Duration {
    storage_api_publish_worst_case(request_timeout)
        .saturating_mul(super::super::store::RECONCILE_GRACE_MULTIPLE)
}

/// `phase` label value on `live_edit_checkpoint_adapter_ceiling_elapsed_total`
/// for a [`storage_api_publish_worst_case`] elapse in `send_classified`.
const CEILING_PHASE_CALL: &str = "call";

/// `phase` label value on `live_edit_checkpoint_adapter_ceiling_elapsed_total`
/// for a [`storage_api_enumeration_ceiling`] elapse in one of the LIST-driven
/// walks.
///
/// `pub(crate)` because the enumeration ceiling is no longer enforced only
/// inside this adapter: the reconciliation pass preemptively bounds its two
/// phase-opening enumerations by its own phase budget (b.gg7 review round 2,
/// `checkpointer::ReconcilePassBudget`), which is the tighter bound, and an
/// elapse there is the same event from the operator's point of view — an
/// enumeration abandoned at a ceiling. One label value, one alert.
pub(crate) const CEILING_PHASE_ENUMERATION: &str = "enumeration";

/// Walk descriptors handed to
/// [`StorageApiCheckpointStore::send_classified_within`].
///
/// Each MUST match the phrasing its walk's own cooperative-deadline error uses.
/// The clamp and the between-calls test are the same ceiling firing a call
/// apart, so an operator (and the tests that pin the distinction between the
/// walks) must not be able to tell them apart by wording — only the walks
/// apart from each other.
const WALK_LAYERS_ENUMERATION: &str = "layers enumeration";
const WALK_SESSIONS_ENUMERATION: &str = "sessions enumeration";
const WALK_EXT_REFS_SIDECAR: &str = "ext-refs sidecar walk";
const WALK_TEMP_REAP: &str = "temp-artifact reap";

/// The `sessions` subtree under `<base_path>/.live-edit/`. One constant for the
/// session-checkpoint address builders and the `sessions/` walk of
/// `list_session_checkpoint_ids`, so the subtree a put writes into is the one
/// the listing enumerates.
const SESSIONS_SUBTREE: &str = "sessions";

/// Query-parameter name and value declaring that a by-address GET wants its
/// payload **inline in the response body**, at any size (b.z22).
///
/// storage-api's read decision is `download_preference`-first and size-second:
/// an explicit `Body` is honoured unconditionally, and only the *unspecified*
/// case consults the deployment's `readRedirectSize` and answers HTTP 300 with a
/// presigned redirect instead (`storage-core/src/azure/fileobject/mod.rs`
/// `read_internal`, the `match download_preference` at ~:198-210; the S3 backend
/// makes the identical decision). Sending no preference is therefore what made
/// every artifact above 256 KiB unreadable on dev — the adapter classified the
/// 300 as `Permanent` and DR restore of any large layer failed.
///
/// Declaring `body` restores the design's own premise rather than adding a new
/// one: `LIVE_EDIT_CHECKPOINT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS` was sized at 30 s precisely
/// because checkpoint artifacts are multi-MB inline reads (SR-4.2), and the
/// server streams a body-mode read rather than buffering it. It also keeps
/// reads off an egress path — the presigned target — that this service has
/// never otherwise exercised, and that a cluster's NetworkPolicy / DNS may not
/// permit at all.
///
/// The wire form is the lowercase enum name: the REST layer deserializes
/// `ReadByAddressQueryParams { download_preference: Option<DownloadPreference> }`
/// and the prost enum carries `#[serde(rename_all = "lowercase")]`
/// (`storage-core-protos/build.rs:66-72`); storage-api's own REST client sends
/// the same literal (`storage-core-test/src/clients/rest/fileobject/v1alpha.rs:87-97`).
const DOWNLOAD_PREFERENCE_PARAM: &str = "download_preference";
const DOWNLOAD_PREFERENCE_BODY: &str = "body";

/// Query-parameter name carrying the on-wire byte count of a by-address PUT.
/// Non-optional on the server's `WriteByAddressQueryParams`
/// (`v1alpha/types.rs:67-73`) and, since b.z22, the value storage-api routes
/// the write on — so it must stay accurate even on the re-issued PUT whose
/// body is deliberately empty.
const DATA_OBJECT_SIZE_PARAM: &str = "data_object_size";

/// Query-parameter name and value forcing a by-address PUT down the
/// presigned-redirect upload path (b.z22).
///
/// Used **only** to convert a server-proposed *multipart* upload into a plain
/// redirect upload. storage-api's write decision (`azure/fileobject/mod.rs`
/// `write_internal`, ~:372-397) is asymmetric in a way that matters here:
///
/// - No preference (what this adapter sends by default) → size-routed:
///   inline below `writeBodyMaxSize`, redirect below `writeRedirectMaxSize`,
///   multipart above it.
/// - `upload_preference=body` above `writeBodyMaxSize` → **forced to
///   multipart**, which is strictly worse than what we would have got by
///   staying silent. Never send it.
/// - `upload_preference=redirect` → honoured **unconditionally**, at any size.
///
/// So the one preference worth stating is `redirect`, and only once the server
/// has already told us it wants multipart. Everything else rides the default
/// size routing.
const UPLOAD_PREFERENCE_PARAM: &str = "upload_preference";
const UPLOAD_PREFERENCE_REDIRECT: &str = "redirect";

/// Path suffix of the write-redirect completion handshake, appended to an
/// artifact's `by-address` URL:
/// `POST /v1alpha/fileobject/by-address/{address}/redirect/complete`
/// (`storage-core-builder/src/api_services/fileobject/v1alpha/rest.rs:63-64`).
///
/// Safe to append by string concatenation because
/// [`StorageApiCheckpointStore::by_address_url`] percent-encodes the whole
/// address into a *single* path segment, so nothing in the address can be
/// mistaken for the two segments that follow.
const REDIRECT_COMPLETE_SUFFIX: &str = "/redirect/complete";

/// Path of the storage-api `by-address` route, and of the upload-options
/// pre-flight that shares its address encoding
/// (`storage-core-builder/src/api_services/fileobject/v1alpha/rest.rs:43-49`
/// and `:71-74`).
///
/// Note the pre-flight's **real** path carries the `fileobject/` segment; the
/// `#[utoipa::path]` annotation above its handler (`rest.rs:799`) omits it and
/// is wrong. The axum `Router` is authoritative and is what this adapter
/// matches.
const BY_ADDRESS_PATH: &str = "/v1alpha/fileobject/by-address/";
const UPLOAD_OPTIONS_PATH: &str = "/v1alpha/fileobject/upload-options/by-address/";

/// How long the adapter waits before re-probing the upload-options route after
/// a failed pre-flight (b.z22 review round 1, F3), and — since review round 3
/// — how long an answer that has already been contradicted once stays on
/// probation.
///
/// Sized against the checkpoint cadence rather than against the route: at the
/// default `LIVE_EDIT_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL_SECS = 60` this costs at most one
/// wasted probe per five checkpoint ticks on a deployment whose storage-api
/// predates the route (404) or does not grant `FetchWriteTypeInfo` (403), while
/// still picking up an upgraded server within minutes rather than at the next
/// pod restart.
///
/// One constant serves both roles deliberately: a route that cannot answer and
/// a route whose answer its own server contradicts are the same operational
/// state — "the pre-flight is not currently informative" — and the cadence
/// argument above applies unchanged to both. See [`UploadOptions`].
const UPLOAD_OPTIONS_REPROBE_BACKOFF: Duration = Duration::from_secs(300);

/// Payload size at or above which a write consults the upload-options
/// pre-flight (b.z22 review round 1, F3).
///
/// The pre-flight exists so a *large* write does not speculatively stream a
/// payload the server will not read. Below this size that speculation is not
/// worth a round trip to avoid: a publish issues three artifacts, two of which
/// (the meta and the ext-refs sidecar) are a few hundred bytes, and no shipped
/// deployment routes a write this small anywhere but inline — 256 KiB is the
/// *read* redirect threshold on dev, and the write threshold sits an order of
/// magnitude above it at 2 MiB. So a sub-threshold write's speculative body is
/// bounded by this constant and is almost always the body the server wanted
/// anyway.
///
/// Being wrong here is not a correctness failure, only a missed optimisation: a
/// deployment configured with `writeBodyMaxSize` below this bound would answer
/// 300 to a small write, and the reactive path handles it exactly as it did
/// before the pre-flight existed (and drops the cache via
/// [`StorageApiCheckpointStore::invalidate_upload_options`] so the *next* write
/// re-probes once, and a deployment that keeps contradicting itself stops being
/// asked at all).
const UPLOAD_OPTIONS_MIN_PAYLOAD_BYTES: usize = 256 * 1024;

/// `verb` label values on `live_edit_checkpoint_adapter_redirect_total`.
const REDIRECT_VERB_GET: &str = "get";
const REDIRECT_VERB_PUT: &str = "put";

/// Call-site descriptors handed to [`multiple_choices_message`] (b.z22 review
/// round 1, F5).
///
/// Each names the *actual* call site an HTTP 300 arrived on. Before the review
/// every rejecting site shared one message that listed all of them as a
